{
  "image_name": "e1fad2b2c133e61f50b4c3c31827db42.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how different solar-heating designs interact with water and how water transport changes with increasing water content. Overall, it shows that direct contact between a solar absorber and bulk water causes both heat conduction into the water and convective circulation, which can waste heat. In the first schematic, the absorber sits on the water surface, so absorbed solar energy is readily conducted downward and drives strong convection. Adding an insulating layer reduces this heat loss, while a contactless configuration further separates the absorber from the water, apparently minimizing direct conductive and convective losses while still allowing thermal radiation outward.\n\nThe lower row shifts from heat flow to water movement inside porous or absorbent structures. At low water content, transport is mainly one-dimensional and slow, with water rising vertically. As water content increases, transport becomes two-dimensional and then three-dimensional, with more branched, circulating pathways and faster movement throughout the volume. The sequence suggests that higher water content enables more connected pathways for liquid redistribution, changing from simple upward flow to complex internal circulation. Taken together, the figure emphasizes strategies to localize solar heating and improve water supply while suppressing unwanted heat dissipation into bulk water.",
